Grid is the right choice for two-dimensional layouts (rows AND columns).
.gallery {
display: grid;
grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(220px, 1fr));
gap: 1rem;
}
That one declaration creates a responsive image grid: as many columns as fit at minimum 220px each, the rest fluid.
Common patterns:
/* 12-column layout */
.container { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(12, 1fr); gap: 1rem; }
.hero { grid-column: 1 / -1; }
.sidebar { grid-column: 1 / span 3; }
.main { grid-column: 4 / -1; }
/* named areas */
.layout {
display: grid;
grid-template-areas:
"header header"
"nav main"
"footer footer";
grid-template-columns: 200px 1fr;
}
.header { grid-area: header; }
.nav { grid-area: nav; }
.main { grid-area: main; }
.footer { grid-area: footer; }
Trick: Grid for the page skeleton, Flexbox for the components inside it. Grid handles "where do regions sit on the page?". Flexbox handles "how do icons + text align inside this button?".

The mobile-first pattern: write base styles for small screens, add complexity at larger breakpoints with @media.
/* base: mobile */
.card { padding: 1rem; }
/* tablet up */
@media (min-width: 768px) {
.card { padding: 1.5rem; }
}
/* desktop up */
@media (min-width: 1024px) {
.card { padding: 2rem; max-width: 1100px; margin: 0 auto; }
}
Standard breakpoints (rough): 480 (large phone), 768 (tablet), 1024 (laptop), 1280+ (desktop).
Use container queries (@container) in modern browsers when component layout should depend on the container's width, not the viewport's.

